r/>restored.read_file(f"runs/{run_b}/summary.md")   # absent — run 2 didn't exist yet</pre><p>Fork forward from run 2’s snapshot and both appear. Real backward-and-forward time travel over real runs.</p>Gotchas the docs won’t warn you about<p>A handful of things will trip you up if you follow the documentation literally. These are the ones I hit, with the fix baked in:</p><ul><li><strong>I used</strong><strong>fork(), not </strong><strong>restore.</strong> I found fork() is the better path, and honestly the better one (just my opinion).</li><li><strong>Stagger fresh concurrent sessions</strong> (Step 3). Partitioned writes coordinate lock-free once settled, but launching many brand-new sessions at the exact same instant is asking for trouble — a couple seconds of spacing removes it.</li><li>Mount under /home/tl-user, not elsewhere — the default user can’t write outside its home directory, and you don’t need — user root for that. Force an initial commit before a fresh filesystem’s first mount.</li></ul>When to reach for something else<p>Two scope boundaries so you point this at the right problem. This is <strong>working-state reproducibility</strong> — replaying the exact filesystem state of a run — not semantic memory; pair it with a vector store when you need recall-by-meaning. And it’s a <strong>single linear timeline</strong>, not a branching workflow; for agents exploring divergent branches you merge later, Tensorlake’s Git Repositories is the better-fit primitive. Inside its lane — reproduce a run, diff two runs, restore on a fresh box — this is the cleanest approach I’ve used.</p>Your next 30 minutes<p>Start tiny: pip install tensorlake, then run Step 1 and Step 5 alone — create a filesystem, write a file, snapshot it, read it back. Under five minutes, no mount.</p><p>Then wire Steps 2–4 with two dummy items and a single cheap LLM call per worker, and watch two subtrees land in one coherent tree. Once that works, run it twice at different temperatures and diff the summaries. The first time Step 6 shows you exactly what one prompt change did across five agents at once, reproducibility stops being the chore you keep meaning to add — and becomes something your pipeline just has.</p>References<p>[1] Tensorlake — Sandboxes for AI Agents] ( <a href="https://www.tensorlake.ai/?utm_source=medium&amp;utm_medium=sponsored_content&amp;utm_campaign=darshan_aug2026">https://www.tensorlake.ai/?utm_source=medium&amp;utm_medium=sponsored_content&amp;utm_campaign=darshan_aug2026</a> )</p><p>[2] Zylos Research, “Durable Execution for AI Agent Runtimes: Checkpointing, Replay, and Recovery,” April 2026. <a href="https://zylos.ai/research/2026-04-24-durable-execution-agent-runtimes/">https://zylos.ai/research/2026-04-24-durable-execution-agent-runtimes/</a></p><p>[3] Freestyle, “Version Control for AI Agents,” May 2026. <a href="https://www.freestyle.sh/blog/engineering/version-control-for-ai-agents">https://www.freestyle.sh/blog/engineering/version-control-for-ai-agents</a></p><p>[4] “From Prompt–Response to Goal-Directed Systems: The Evolution of Agentic AI Software Architecture,” arXiv, Feb 2026. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2602.10479v1">https://arxiv.org/html/2602.10479v1</a></p><p>[5] P. Enberg, “Towards a Disaggregated Agent Filesystem on Object Storage.” <a href="https://penberg.org/blog/disaggregated-agentfs.html">https://penberg.org/blog/disaggregated-agentfs.html</a></p><p>[6] Tensorlake Documentation — Sandboxes (<a href="https://docs.tensorlake.ai/sandboxes/introduction?utm_source=medium&amp;utm_medium=sponsored_content&amp;utm_campaign=darshan_aug2026">https://docs.tensorlake.ai/sandboxes/introduction?utm_source=medium&amp;utm_medium=sponsored_content&amp;utm_campaign=darshan_aug2026</a>)</p><p><a href="https://pub.towardsai.net/build-a-reproducible-multi-agent-pipeline-on-a-versioned-filesystem-9d8c15dcf093">Build a Reproducible Multi-Agent Pipeline on a Versioned Filesystem</a> was originally published in <a href="https://